# 4. Modeling Methodology

We will use two different cross-validation schemes:

* **10-fold CV (repeated 3x)**: For MLR, Random Forest, and GBM
* **5-fold CV**: For KNN, Ridge Regression, and Elastic Net

```{r train-control}
# 10-fold cross-validation, repeated 3 times
ctrl_10fold <- trainControl(
  method  = "repeatedcv",
  number  = 10,
  repeats = 3,
  savePredictions = "final",
  verboseIter = FALSE
)

# 5-fold cross-validation (for new models)
ctrl_5fold <- trainControl(
  method  = "cv",
  number  = 5,
  savePredictions = "final",
  verboseIter = FALSE
)
```

---

# 5. Model Development (10-Fold CV Models)

## 5.1 Model 1: Multiple Linear Regression

Multiple linear regression serves as our baseline model. It provides interpretable coefficients that help explain the relationship between manufacturing parameters and pH levels.

### 5.1.1 Model Training

```{r mlr-model}
# Train multiple linear regression model
mlr_model <- train(
  ph ~ .,
  data = train_clean,
  method = "lm",
  trControl = ctrl_10fold,
  na.action = na.omit
)

# Model summary
print(mlr_model)

# View coefficients
mlr_summary <- summary(mlr_model$finalModel)
print(mlr_summary)
```

### 5.1.2 Feature Importance

```{r mlr-importance}
# Variable importance based on t-statistics
mlr_importance <- varImp(mlr_model, scale = TRUE)
plot(mlr_importance, top = 15, main = "MLR Variable Importance")
```

### 5.1.3 Diagnostic Plots

```{r mlr-diagnostics}
# Residual plots
par(mfrow = c(2, 2))
plot(mlr_model$finalModel)
par(mfrow = c(1, 1))
```

### 5.1.4 Cross-Validation Performance

```{r mlr-cv-performance}
# Cross-validation results
mlr_cv_results <- mlr_model$results
print("MLR Cross-Validation Results (10-fold, 3 repeats):")
print(mlr_cv_results)

cat("\nMLR Cross-Validation RMSE:", round(mlr_cv_results$RMSE, 4))
cat("\nMLR Cross-Validation R-squared:", round(mlr_cv_results$Rsquared, 4))
```

---

## 5.2 Model 2: Random Forest

Random Forest is an ensemble method that builds multiple decision trees and aggregates their predictions.

### 5.2.1 Model Training with Hyperparameter Tuning

```{r rf-model}
# Define tuning grid for mtry (number of variables at each split)
rf_grid <- expand.grid(
  mtry = c(2, 4, 6, 8, 10, floor(sqrt(length(predictor_vars))))
)

# Train Random Forest model
rf_model <- train(
  ph ~ .,
  data = train_clean,
  method = "rf",
  trControl = ctrl_10fold,
  tuneGrid = rf_grid,
  ntree = 500,
  importance = TRUE,
  na.action = na.omit
)

# Model summary
print(rf_model)

# Best tuning parameters
cat("\nBest mtry:", rf_model$bestTune$mtry)
```

### 5.2.2 Hyperparameter Tuning Visualization

```{r rf-tuning}
# Plot tuning results
plot(rf_model, main = "Random Forest Hyperparameter Tuning")
```

### 5.2.3 Variable Importance

```{r rf-importance}
# Variable importance
rf_importance <- varImp(rf_model, scale = TRUE)
plot(rf_importance, top = 15, main = "Random Forest Variable Importance")

# Detailed importance from the final model
importance_df <- as.data.frame(importance(rf_model$finalModel))
importance_df$Variable <- rownames(importance_df)
importance_df <- importance_df %>%
  arrange(desc(`%IncMSE`)) %>%
  head(15)

print("Top 15 Important Variables (% Increase in MSE):")
print(importance_df[, c("Variable", "%IncMSE", "IncNodePurity")])
```

### 5.2.4 Cross-Validation Performance

```{r rf-cv-performance}
# Cross-validation results
rf_cv_results <- rf_model$results %>% 
  filter(mtry == rf_model$bestTune$mtry)

print("Random Forest Cross-Validation Results (10-fold, 3 repeats):")
print(rf_cv_results)

cat("\nRF Cross-Validation RMSE:", round(rf_cv_results$RMSE, 4))
cat("\nRF Cross-Validation R-squared:", round(rf_cv_results$Rsquared, 4))
```

